<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nabis</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 192 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · Also known as Nabis of Sparta</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nabis was the last ruler of independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>, from 207 to 192 BCE. He carried out radical reforms, freeing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helots">helots</a>, redistributing land and enlarging the citizen body, and gave Sparta its first full city walls. Defeated by a Roman and Achaean coalition under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/titus-quinctius-flamininus">Flamininus</a> in 195 BCE, he lost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argo">Argos</a> and his coastal towns. He was murdered by his Aetolian allies in 192 BCE, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philopoemen">Philopoemen</a> then brought Sparta into the Achaean League.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nabis.jpg" alt="Nabis" width="320" height="164" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: British Museum · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nabis,_tetradrachm,_206-197_BC,_HGC-609.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
